<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashkelon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ascalon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashkelon is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of southern Israel and one of the oldest seaports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. A Philistine city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, it later flourished under Hellenistic, Roman and Islamic rule. As Ascalon it was the last Fatimid stronghold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> until King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin III of Jerusalem</a> captured it in 1153; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> dismantled it in 1191, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> razed it in 1270.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashkelon.jpg" alt="Ashkelon" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: דוידי ורדי · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:93439_marina_ashkelon_PikiWiki_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
